  "worked_solution": "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y\\ne z.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations.\n\n- Relation $R$ is not transitive. Notice that \n\n- $(-4,-6)\\in R$ since $-4+(-6)=-10<0,$ and\n\n- $(-6,5)\\in R$ since $-6+5=-1<0,$ but\n\n- $(-4,5)\\notin R$ since $-4+5=1≮0.$\n\n- Relation $S$ is not transitive. Notice that \n\n- $(-2,4)\\in S$ since $-2+4=2,$ and\n\n- $(4,-2)\\in S$ since $4+(-2)=2,$ but\n\n- $(-2,-2)\\notin S$ since $-2+(-2)=-4\\ne 2.$\n\n- Relation $T$ is not transitive. Notice that \n\n- $(0,2)\\in T$ since $0≡2+1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3),$ and\n\n- $(2,4)\\in T$ since $2≡4+1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3),$ but\n\n- $(0,4)\\notin T$ since $0≢4+1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\"",
